SELECT i.slug,'Government','Abbott Appoints John Mason to Texas One-Call Utility Safety Board',
'The June 24 appointment initially ran through August 31, 2026; a later September reappointment is documented separately. The original article copy is unavailable.',
NULL::text,'Keep TX Red Newsroom','Office of the Texas Governor','https://gov.texas.gov/news/post/governor-abbott-appoints-mason-to-one-call-board-of-texas',
'2026-06-28T12:00:00Z'::timestamptz,'news','{"updated":"2026-10-14T19:42:00Z","intro":["Governor Greg Abbott appointed John Mason of Dallas to the One-Call Board of Texas on June 24, 2026, for a term scheduled to expire August 31, 2026. The board oversees the notification system that helps protect buried utility lines before excavation work begins. The decision was a public infrastructure-safety appointment, not the separate June 29 appointment of Sean Mason to lead Texas'' Office of State-Federal Relations.","The original Keep TX Red article bearing this June 28 address is unavailable in the surviving database. This reconstruction draws on the governor''s contemporary announcement, the One-Call Board''s description of its responsibilities, and the subsequent September board appointment record. Its wording is new; the historical URL is preserved."],"sections":[{"heading":"Who was appointed and for how long","paragraphs":["Abbott''s June 24 announcement identified John Mason as assistant vice president and senior legal counsel at AT&T. The governor''s biography said Mason had previously worked as a managing attorney for the Public Utility Commission of Texas. His career therefore included both a regulated communications utility and an agency whose work intersects with utility service and infrastructure.","The announced term was explicitly limited: it was set to expire August 31, 2026. That date matters when reading an archival article months later. The original appointment should not be described as an indefinite term or used to infer membership after August without checking a newer official record. On September 15, the governor separately announced Mason''s reappointment through August 31, 2029, alongside other members. That later action confirms continuity but is not the event originally reported in June."]},{"heading":"What the One-Call Board actually oversees","paragraphs":["The One-Call Board of Texas exists to support the statewide system commonly described as ''Call Before You Dig.'' The board''s own guidance describes a process in which a person planning excavation notifies the system before work, which then alerts the underground facility operators that may have buried utilities in the work area. Those operators arrange the location and marking of underground lines as applicable.","That process can reduce accidental contact with gas pipelines, electrical cables, telecommunications facilities and other infrastructure. A strike can interrupt service, endanger workers and residents, and produce expensive repair work. Notification is not a guarantee that every underground condition is known, and an excavator must still use required safe practices around marked lines."]},{"heading":"Why board membership has public-policy consequences","paragraphs":["Board appointments influence oversight of the notification framework, which brings excavators, utility operators and the designated notification center into one process. The system affects contractors and infrastructure construction crews, but homeowners planning to install fencing or dig for landscaping may also have responsibilities under Texas excavation law.","The board''s guidance explains that the Texas 811 service is designed to notify relevant facility operators and is supported by utilities. Its regulatory-authority page also distinguishes the board''s direct oversight of the registered notification center from enforcement powers exercised by other public authorities. That distinction is important: membership on the One-Call Board does not itself give an appointee unrestricted power to impose penalties for every excavation dispute."]},{"heading":"Mason''s prior public and professional experience","paragraphs":["Beyond his AT&T legal position, the official June biography listed service at the Public Utility Commission and participation in Dallas-area legal and volunteer programs. It cited a degree in government from the University of Texas at Austin and a law degree from St. Mary''s School of Law. Those are biographical statements published by the governor''s office, not independent findings about how he would vote on future board matters.","Experience in the utilities sector may be useful for understanding operational and legal requirements, but an appointment announcement alone cannot establish the performance or independence of a future board member. The record shows who was selected and the term assigned. Actual board decisions should be assessed against official meeting materials and actions rather than an implied endorsement."]},{"heading":"The separate September reappointment","paragraphs":["On September 15, the governor announced new One-Call Board terms for several members, including John Mason. Mason, Marcela Navarette and Keith Wall were identified as reappointed, Michael Holdman as appointed through August 2029, and Michael McCloskey as appointed through August 2027. This later record is included because a historical June article''s short term had already expired by the reconstruction date.","The September action should not be folded into the July-era headline as though it happened at the same time. The June 24 appointment and September 15 reappointment are two official acts with different terms. Keeping that sequence clear is more useful than recycling the same biography without updating the officeholder''s status."]},{"heading":"Where Texans can verify excavation rules","paragraphs":["The One-Call Board''s official site explains the practical steps for contacting Texas 811 and the regulatory authorities relevant to different underground utility types. Details of notice periods, excavation depth, exceptions and enforcement can depend on the circumstances and current state law. Before breaking ground, homeowners and contractors should follow the current official instructions rather than treating this historical appointment article as legal guidance.","This article was reconstructed on October 14, 2026 from the identified records because Keep TX Red''s original June 28 copy could not be found in the database or repository. It has not been represented as a verbatim recovery, and no unrelated portrait is used to imply that a photograph depicts the appointee or appointment ceremony."]}],"faq":[{"q":"Which Mason did Governor Abbott appoint to the One-Call Board?","a":"John Mason, a Dallas-based AT&T senior legal counsel and former Public Utility Commission attorney. Sean Mason was a different official appointed to another office on June 29."},{"q":"When did John Mason''s original term end?","a":"The June 24 announcement identified August 31, 2026. Abbott announced a separate September 15 reappointment through August 31, 2029."},{"q":"What is Texas 811?","a":"It is the statewide excavation-notification system that helps utilities identify and mark underground lines before digging."}],"sources":[{"label":"Office of the Texas Governor — June 24 John Mason appointment","url":"https://gov.texas.gov/news/post/governor-abbott-appoints-mason-to-one-call-board-of-texas"},{"label":"One-Call Board of Texas — how notification works","url":"https://onecalltexas.com/how-it-works/"},{"label":"One-Call Board of Texas — regulatory authorities","url":"https://onecalltexas.com/regulatory-authorities/"},{"label":"Office of the Texas Governor — September 15 board reappointments","url":"https://gov.texas.gov/news/post/governor-abbott-appoints-five-to-one-call-board-of-texas"}],"keyTakeaways":["Abbott appointed John Mason to the One-Call Board on June 24, 2026, with an initial term ending August 31.","The board helps oversee Texas 811 excavation notification, while enforcement duties can fall to other agencies.","A separate September 15 release reappointed John Mason; he is not Sean Mason of the Office of State-Federal Relations."]}'::jsonb,
